 S~S  SimpleSal workspace model documents: README information about workspace models

=====================

 S~S TruSoft Computing LLC (TSC LLC) SimpleSal Software Developer Workspace Assumptions

------------------------------------------

 S~S   SimpleSal is designed to peacefully co-exist within any development environment

    The contents of the repo is a set of text files, numbered in suggested order of viewing.
    The text files refer to a set of image (.PNG) files, each showing a repo task sequence.

    The "model" is the organization of the files the developer is working on with an editor.
    The "workspace" is an implementation of a model by a developer in order to edit files.
    "Instances" are specific collections of files as defined by the repository database.
    "Mediums" are versions of software that have come from or are going into the repository.
    "Repositories" are databases accessible through the medium with data about the content.

	This is only SimpleSal's view of the world; there is always a model, for good reasons.

------------------------------------------

=====================

------------------------------------------

  S~S The Elevator Pitch

------------------------------------------

    This README file is about the documentation provided through GitHub explaining the model
    a consumer of SimpleSal software and documentation can use to navigate the content.

    The implementation of a model requires choices about file types, technical style,
    information detail level, vocabulary formality; these are all determined by the culture.

    SimpleSal is focused on the developer being introduced to the idea, the content, and the
    implementation of a moderately complex model sufficient and necessary for common tasks.

    The contents of this repo address that moderately sophisticated developer in GitHub terms.

------------------------------------------

=====================

 S~S The SimpleSal Ecosystem

------------------------------------------

  S~S The value of a 3-legged footstool

    What is the increase in complexity when moving from three legs to four legs?  It is the
    addition of the fourth leg that creates a need in the design for parallel lines and symmetry.
    The four-legged footstool wobbles usually; the three-legged footstool is stable anywhere.
    It probably is never truly level across the surface, but it is stable to stand on.

    The SimpleSal three-legged stool is built from:

    1) the published software in the repo:
        GitHub username SimpleSal, simplesal repository, branch main-simplesal

    2) the information within the published software about how to make use of SimpleSal software:
        \SimpleSal\ssDocs\Find and Open Documents.html          <web browser viewable>

    3) the information about how to write signal algorithms using software
        simplesal's substack.com website, most content free without a subscription, see below

------------------------------------------

 S~S Published information outside the repo's human metadata AND repo metadata.

    This text is not-clickable information about the free SimpleSal ecosystem of code and documents.

    The site includes pretty documents with colorful drawings constructed from circles and arrows.

        Also, videos showing the effect of the annotated software being shown alongside the effect.

    simplesal.substack.com          user "simplesal" on site "substack.com"

